{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Help{% endblock %}
{% block page_title %}Help{% endblock %}

{#
 # A tile linking to a page of Forger.
 #}
{% macro tile(route, icon, title, description) %}
    {% import '_macros/tiles.html.twig' as tiles %}
    {{ tiles.tile(path(route), icon, title, description) }}
{% endmacro %}

{% block body %}
    {% import _self as help %}
    {% import '_macros/status.html.twig' as status %}

    {% set sections = [
        {id: 'data', icon: 'actions-database', title: 'Where the data comes from'},
        {id: 'dashboard', icon: 'actions-viewmode-tiles', title: 'Dashboard'},
        {id: 'my-work', icon: 'actions-user', title: 'My Work'},
        {id: 'reviews', icon: 'actions-code-pull-request', title: 'Reviews'},
        {id: 'issues', icon: 'actions-list', title: 'Issues'},
        {id: 'activity', icon: 'actions-clock', title: 'Activity'},
        {id: 'boards', icon: 'actions-line-columns', title: 'Boards'},
        {id: 'tools', icon: 'actions-briefcase', title: 'Tools'},
        {id: 'get-involved', icon: 'actions-heart', title: 'Get involved'},
        {id: 'filters', icon: 'actions-filter', title: 'Filters and search'},
        {id: 'api', icon: 'actions-code', title: 'API'},
    ] %}

    <div class="content-section bg-transparent">
        <div class="container help">
            <nav class="help-toc" aria-label="Contents">
                <div class="help-toc-title">On this page</div>
                <ul>
                    {% for section in sections %}
                        <li><a href="#{{ section.id }}">{{ icon(section.icon) }}<span>{{ section.title }}</span></a></li>
                    {% endfor %}
                </ul>
            </nav>

            <div class="help-content">
                <div class="help-intro">
                    <p>
                        Forger shows the work on the TYPO3 Core in one place: the issues of Forge and the changes on Gerrit,
                        sorted into lists that answer <em>what needs a review?</em>, <em>what can be merged?</em>
                        and <em>what has nobody touched for months?</em>
                    </p>
                </div>

                {# Where the data comes from #}
                <section id="data" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-database') }}</span>
                        <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
                    </header>
                    <div class="help-sources">
                        <div class="help-source">
                            <div class="help-source-title">Forge</div>
                            <p>The issues of the project <a href="https://forge.typo3.org/projects/typo3cms-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TYPO3 Core</a>, without subprojects, private issues and private notes.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="help-source">
                            <div class="help-source-title">Gerrit</div>
                            <p>The changes of <a href="https://review.typo3.org/q/project:Packages/TYPO3.CMS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Packages/TYPO3.CMS</a> with their votes, patch sets and files.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="help-muted">
                        Forger copies the data in regular imports, so a change shows up after the next one has run.
                        Every issue and change links to its original.
                    </p>
                </section>

                {# Dashboard #}
                <section id="dashboard" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-viewmode-tiles') }}</span>
                        <h2>Dashboard</h2>
                    </header>
                    <dl class="help-facts">
                        <div><dt>Open issues</dt><dd>with those opened and closed in the last 30 days</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Open Gerrit changes</dt><dd>mergeable, with a conflict or work in progress</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>Waiting over a month</dt><dd>ready for review, no update for 30 days</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                    <p>
                        Logged in, you see "For you" below the figures: what waits for you, what of your interests
                        you can review, and where the people you follow need help. Each card shows the first three to
                        act on, "Show all" opens its lists in <a href="#my-work">My Work</a>. A change or an issue
                        shows in one card only.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Below the figures, "What needs attention" lists the changes ready to merge, the changes that
                        wait for a review for over a month, the open regressions, as Forge marks them with "Is
                        Regression", and the new issues.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The <a href="{{ path('dashboard_statistics') }}">statistics</a> are one board of all the
                        activity in a time window: the key figures of Forge and Gerrit, how long work waits, what came
                        in and went out, the new contributors, who carries the review and when people are active. A
                        dashed line marks the median of a chart. Below, the open issues and changes over three years
                        and what was closed per year. Each chart switches to a table.
                    </p>
                    <p class="help-muted">The history is worked out from when issues and changes were created and closed, so it is close to, not exactly, what Forge and Gerrit showed back then.</p>
                </section>

                {# My Work #}
                <section id="my-work" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-user') }}</span>
                        <h2>My Work</h2>
                    </header>
                    <p>
                        Log in with your TYPO3 account to see your own work. Forger finds it by the accounts, the
                        email address and the name of your account. You reach every part from "My Work" in the menu
                        and from the menu of your name.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="help-points">
                        <li>
                            <strong>Overview</strong> shows a few of each list: your changes that wait for you, the
                            issues assigned to you, the work of your interests, and where the people you follow need
                            help. "Show all" opens the search with the same filters, so you see all of it there.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Reviews</strong> and <strong>Issues</strong> list your changes and your issues.
                            Closed ones are hidden until you choose "Include closed".
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Interests</strong> are the areas you care for, like the documentation. An interest
                            has paths of files, like <code>*.rst</code>, and categories of Forge. It shows the open
                            changes that touch any of the paths and that you did not vote on yet, and the new issues of
                            its categories. Without categories it shows the new issues of all categories.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Following</strong> shows where the people you follow need help: their changes that
                            are stuck, their changes without a review, and their issues that are still new after a week.
                            Below it is what they did in the last 30 days.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Only you see whom you follow. The people you follow get no notice. You can follow up to
                            {{ constant('App\\Repository\\FollowRepository::LIMIT') }} people.
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <p id="person-cards">
                        <strong>Person cards.</strong> "People you follow" shows each person as a card with what the
                        person did in the last {{ constant('App\\People\\PeopleActivity::RANGE').label }}. The
                        figures count as the <a href="{{ path('dashboard_statistics') }}">statistics</a> do, without
                        the events of bots. The name opens the activity of the person in the same time.
                    </p>
                    <dl class="help-facts">
                        <div><dt>reviews</dt><dd>votes and comments in Gerrit</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>patch sets</dt><dd>new changes and new patch sets in Gerrit</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>issues</dt><dd>issues created on Forge</dd></div>
                        <div><dt>comments</dt><dd>comments on Forge issues</dd></div>
                    </dl>
                    <p>The line below the figures shows all events of the person per week.</p>
                </section>

                {# Reviews #}
                <section id="reviews" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-code-pull-request') }}</span>
                        <h2>Reviews</h2>
                    </header>
                    <p>
                        The lists show open changes, the ones updated last first. Each list can be sorted and searched by subject, commit message, number, Change-Id, topic and owner.
                        Work in progress only appears in its own list and in the search. The filter "File" finds the changes that touch a file, by its path, or any file of a folder.
                        Votes count when a person gave them. Of the CI bot only a red build counts, as Verified -1: it keeps the change from being merged.
                    </p>
                    <div class="link-tiles">
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_ready', 'actions-code-pull-request', 'Ready for review', 'Every open change that is not work in progress, has no negative vote and no merge conflict.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_wip', 'actions-code-pull-request-draft', 'Work in progress', 'Marked as work in progress in Gerrit or with [WIP] or [POC] in the subject, the oldest first.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_needs_review', 'actions-comment', 'Needs review', 'Verified, but no positive code review yet.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_needs_verification', 'actions-check-circle', 'Needs verification', 'A positive code review, but not verified yet.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_needs_rebase', 'actions-refresh', 'Needs rebase', 'Gerrit cannot merge them because of a conflict.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_negative_votes', 'actions-minus-circle', 'Negative votes', 'At least one negative review or verification.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_verified', 'actions-check-badge', 'Reviewed and verified', 'A positive review and verification, whatever else they have.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('reviews_ready_to_merge', 'actions-code-merge', 'Ready to merge', 'Reviewed and verified, no negative vote, no conflict.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('search_changes', 'actions-search', 'Search', 'Words find open changes, also work in progress. The subject leads to a page with the commit message, the issues and the files of a change.') }}
                    </div>
                    <p id="change-sizes">
                        The size of a change tells how long its review takes, like the difficulty of a game: the lines it adds and removes together, as Gerrit counts them.
                        The filter "Size" of the lists shows the changes of one or more sizes, "Smallest first" in their sort field puts the quick reviews on top.
                    </p>
                    <div class="help-stages help-stages-sizes">
                        {% for size in enum_cases('App\\Enum\\ReviewSizeEnum') %}
                            <div class="help-stage">
                                {{ status.sizePill(size, size.description) }}
                                <span class="help-stage-range">
                                    {% if size.maximum is null %}
                                        over {{ size.minimum - 1 }} lines
                                    {% elseif size.minimum == 0 %}
                                        up to {{ size.maximum }} lines
                                    {% else %}
                                        {{ size.minimum }}–{{ size.maximum }} lines
                                    {% endif %}
                                </span>
                            </div>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </div>
                </section>

                {# Issues #}
                <section id="issues" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-list') }}</span>
                        <h2>Issues</h2>
                    </header>
                    <div class="link-tiles">
                        {{ help.tile('search_search', 'actions-search', 'Search', 'Words find open issues and reviews, each in a tab. A number shows the issue and the change with it, and finds the changes that resolve the issue. The page of an issue shows its changes and finds its possible duplicates.') }}
                        <div id="stale-issues" class="link-tile-anchor">
                            {{ help.tile('issues_stale', 'actions-clock', 'Stale issues', 'Open issues without activity for 60 days or more, the stages "Stale" and "Orphaned". Each says which changes it has, linked in the comments or resolving or naming the issue in their commit message.') }}
                        </div>
                        {{ help.tile('issues_duplicates', 'actions-duplicates', 'Possible duplicates', 'Pairs of open issues with much of their wording in common, checked from both sides, the most similar first. Pairs Forge already relates are left out.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('issues_by_category', 'actions-tag', 'By category', 'Open issues per category and tracker, the categories with the most first.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('issues_by_month', 'actions-calendar', 'By month', 'Open issues by the month they were created: how old the open issues are.') }}
                    </div>
                    <p class="help-muted">Tick "Include closed" in the search to include resolved, closed and rejected issues, or merged and abandoned changes. Admins refresh an issue or a change from its page.</p>
                </section>

                {# Activity #}
                <section id="activity" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-clock') }}</span>
                        <h2>Activity</h2>
                    </header>
                    <p>
                        Open changes and issues show how long ago they were last updated: a comment, a vote, a new patch set or a new status.
                        Bots on Gerrit only vote on uploaded patch sets, so they do not make a change look busy.
                    </p>
                    <div class="help-stages">
                        {% for stage in enum_cases('App\\Enum\\ActivityEnum') %}
                            <div class="help-stage">
                                {{ status.activityPill(stage, null, null) }}
                                <span class="help-stage-range">
                                    {% if stage.maximumDays is null %}
                                        over {{ stage.minimumDays }} days
                                    {% elseif stage.minimumDays == 0 %}
                                        up to {{ stage.maximumDays }} days
                                    {% else %}
                                        {{ stage.minimumDays }}–{{ stage.maximumDays }} days
                                    {% endif %}
                                </span>
                            </div>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </div>
                    <ul class="help-points">
                        <li>Merged, abandoned, resolved, closed and rejected ones have no stage, and the "Activity" filter never counts them.</li>
                        <li>Issues "On Hold" or "Postponed" are open, so they get a stage like any other.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="help-callout">
                        <strong>Not the same as "Stale issues".</strong>
                        The stage is about time only. The list <a href="#stale-issues">Stale issues</a> shows issues under review without an open change.
                    </div>
                </section>

                {# Boards #}
                <section id="boards" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-line-columns') }}</span>
                        <h2>Boards</h2>
                    </header>
                    <div class="link-tiles">
                        <div class="link-tile link-tile-static">
                            <span class="link-tile-icon">{{ icon('actions-viewmode-tiles') }}</span>
                            <span class="link-tile-body">
                                <span class="link-tile-title">Issue boards</span>
                                <span class="link-tile-text">Selected issues in the columns Blocked, Open, WIP, Review and Done. The Core team creates them.</span>
                            </span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="link-tile link-tile-static">
                            <span class="link-tile-icon">{{ icon('actions-line-columns') }}</span>
                            <span class="link-tile-body">
                                <span class="link-tile-title">Review boards</span>
                                <span class="link-tile-text">Open changes, like the bugfixes, features or tasks, grouped by the votes they lack.</span>
                            </span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                {# Tools #}
                <section id="tools" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-briefcase') }}</span>
                        <h2>Tools</h2>
                    </header>
                    <div class="link-tiles">
                        {{ help.tile('utilities_backport', 'actions-code-compare', 'Backports', 'Merged into main within a year, with a version in "Releases:" that has no backport yet.') }}
                        {{ help.tile('utilities_rst', 'actions-file-text', 'ReST helper', 'Writes the changelog file of a breaking change, deprecation or feature.') }}
                    </div>
                </section>

                {# Get involved #}
                <section id="get-involved" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-heart') }}</span>
                        <h2>Get involved</h2>
                    </header>
                    <p>
                        <a href="{{ path('get_involved') }}">Get involved</a> is the place to start if you are new to
                        the TYPO3 Core. Its first steps, from the account to the first patch, link to the
                        <a href="{{ contribution_guide_url() }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contribution Guide</a>.
                        Below them it lists small changes to test, good first issues, new bugs to confirm and
                        regressions, each with a link to all of them.
                    </p>
                </section>

                {# Filters #}
                <section id="filters" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-filter') }}</span>
                        <h2>Filters and search</h2>
                    </header>
                    <ul class="help-points">
                        <li>The number next to a filter value is how many results it has.</li>
                        <li>Values of one filter add up, different filters narrow each other down: <em>Stale or Orphaned, and on main</em>.</li>
                        <li>The "Type" filter of the review lists uses the tags in the subject, like BUGFIX, TASK, FEATURE, DOCS or !!! for a breaking change.</li>
                        <li>
                            The "Marked as" filter of the issue search finds the open issues that need help most: a
                            regression, something that worked in an earlier version, or a good first issue. A good first
                            issue is easy or trivial in Forge, new or accepted, and nobody is assigned to it.
                        </li>
                        <li>The "Tags" filter of the issue search shows the most used tags of the issues found, and the ones you selected.</li>
                        <li>The filters of people, "Owner" of the review lists and "Author" and "Assigned to" of the issue search, show the ten with the most results. Their search finds the others in the list while you type and filters by the one you pick.</li>
                        <li>
                            The "Waiting for" filter of the changes finds who has to act: "Owner" for a negative vote, a
                            merge conflict or unresolved comments, "First review" for a change nobody reviewed yet, and
                            "Your vote" for the open changes that are not yours and that you did not vote on yet. "Your
                            vote" shows when you are logged in.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            The "File" filter finds the changes that touch all of its paths. Choose "any path" to find
                            the changes that touch one of them.
                        </li>
                        <li>The search in the header finds issues from every page.</li>
                        <li>
                            A text of more than {{ constant('App\\Search\\IssueSearch::TEXT_WORDS') }} words, like a
                            pasted report, finds the issues with the most similar wording, as the duplicates do.
                            So you can find out whether somebody already reported something.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                {# API #}
                <section id="api" class="help-section">
                    <header class="help-section-header">
                        <span class="help-section-icon">{{ icon('actions-code') }}</span>
                        <h2>API</h2>
                    </header>
                    <p>
                        The API gives the issues and changes of Forger as JSON, for scripts and tools. It is read only
                        and needs no login.
                        The <a href="{{ path('app.api_doc') }}">API documentation</a> lists the endpoints, their
                        parameters and the fields they return,
                        the <a href="{{ path('app.api_doc_json') }}">OpenAPI document</a> describes them for tools.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="help-points">
                        <li>
                            <strong>Data.</strong> The API has what the pages have. Forger imports Forge and Gerrit
                            every five minutes, so a new issue or patch set shows up after the next import. The
                            <code>url</code> of each issue and change leads to the original. The API never returns
                            the email addresses Forger keeps to match people. Texts stay as their authors wrote them:
                            a merged commit message names its reviewers with their addresses, as Git does.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Format.</strong> Dates are ISO 8601 in UTC, like
                            <code>2026-11-20T23:40:03Z</code>. A field without a value is <code>null</code>, an
                            empty list is <code>[]</code>. Every object has all of its fields. Other sites may
                            call the API from the browser.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Lists.</strong> A list returns one page and <code>total</code>, the number of
                            all items the search found. <code>limit</code> sets the size of a page, 25 by default
                            and 100 at most, <code>offset</code> the position it starts at. The next page starts
                            at <code>offset + limit</code>. Together they may not exceed 10,000: narrow the search
                            to read further.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Errors.</strong> A failed request returns an object with <code>error</code>, a
                            message for people: <code>400</code> for a parameter value the endpoint does not
                            accept, <code>404</code> for an issue or change Forger does not have, <code>429</code> for
                            too many requests.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Values and age.</strong> <code>breakdown=true</code> lists the statuses,
                            trackers, categories and tags the issues of a search have, with their numbers: with
                            <code>status=all</code> those of all issues. The <code>updatedOn</code> of the first item
                            of <code>sort=updated&amp;status=all</code> tells how far the imports are.
                            <code>tag</code> lists the issues with a tag, by its exact name.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Limits.</strong> An address may send 120 requests a minute. Beyond that the API
                            answers <code>429</code>, and the header <code>Retry-After</code> says in how many seconds
                            it takes the next one. The headers <code>X-RateLimit-Limit</code> and
                            <code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> show the limit and what is left of it. Answers may be
                            cached for a minute, the data changes with the imports only.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Versions.</strong> The path holds the major version, <code>/api/v1</code>.
                            Version 1 only gets new fields and parameters. A change that breaks a client gets a
                            new path.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

{% endblock %}
